on\n  * pp. 129 An Algebraic Framework for Diffie-Hellman Assumptions\n  * pp. 239 Non-malleable Codes from Two-Source Extractors\n  * pp. 289 Linearly Homomorphic Structure-Preserving Signatures and Their Applications\n  * pp. 344 Quantum One-Time Programs\n  * pp. 398 Instantiating Random Oracles via UCEs\n  * pp. 416 Obfuscating Conjunctions\n  * pp. 435 Fully, (Almost) Tightly Secure IBE and Dual System Groups\n  * pp. 461 Function-Private Identity-Based Encryption: Hiding the Function in Functional Encryption\n  * pp. 479 Attribute-Based Encryption for Circuits from Multilinear Maps\n  * pp. 500 Functional Encryption: New Perspectives and Lower Bounds\n  * pp. 519 On the Achievability of Simulation-Based Security for Functional Encryption\n  * pp. 536 How to Run Turing Machines on Encrypted Data--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvaluate projective (monomial-basis) sum-check (eprint 2026/762) on the WHIR prover · Issue #1890 · Plonky3/Plonky3 · GitHub (https://github.com/Plonky3/Plonky3/issues/1890)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The WHIR prover runs its folding sum-check in the evaluation (Lagrange) basis. ... I'd like to take this one (cc @tcoratger, who suggested testing 2026/762 against the Plonky3 WHIR implementation).\n\n# Evaluate projective (monomial-basis) sum-check (eprint 2026/762) on the WHIR prover #1890\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nOpen\n\nOpen\n\nEvaluate projective (monomial-basis) sum-check (eprint 2026/762) on the WHIR prover#1890\n\nCopy link\n\nAssignees\n\n## Description\n\njdodinh\n\nopened on Jun 22, 2026\n\nIssue body actions\n\n### Motivation\n\nThe WHIR prover runs its folding sum-check in the evaluation (Lagrange) basis. The dominant cost of linear-time sum-check is the per-round binding step, which updates every cached value with the verifier challenge `r`. The current kernel interpolates between two stored evaluations, costing one subtraction per value:\n    \n    `p(r, x') = p(0, x') + r * (p(1, x') - p(0, x'))   // multilinear-util/src/poly.rs\n    `\n\nThis binding path is shared: besides the WHIR prover, the recently added multi-stark AIR zerocheck (#1879, #1885) binds through the same `p3-multilinear-util` kernels (`fix_prefix_var_mut` and the pointwise `fix_prefix_var_at`), so a subtraction-free binding would benefit both consumers.\n\nThis repo already sends round messages as `[h(0), h(inf)]` and has the verifier derive `h(1) = claimed_sum - h(0)` (`sumcheck/src/{lagrange,data}.rs`). That is the eval-at-infinity round message from \"Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving\" (eprint 2026/587), which puts the point at infinity into the round message as an extrapolation point.\n\n\"The Sum-Check Protocol over the Monomial Basis, and Other Optimizations\" (https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/762, Dao-Biswas-Eagen-Milson-Papini-Thaler) takes the next step: it moves the point at infinity into the interpolating set itself, summing over the projective hypercube `{0, inf}^n` instead of `{0, 1}^n`. By Corollary 3.2 the binding step then becomes subtraction-free:\n    \n    `p(r, x') = p(0, x') + r * p(inf, x')\n    `\n\nbecause `p(inf, x')` is the leading coefficient (the slope), which is stored directly in the monomial representation rather than recovered by subtraction. Proposition 4.1 quantifies this as `d * (2^n - 1)` field subtractions saved across all rounds for a product of `d` multilinears. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTime-Optimal Interactive Proofs for Circuit Evaluation · Pith Review (https://pith.science/paper/1304.3812)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.5 years ago; Crawled: last week; # Time-Optimal Interactive Proofs for Circuit Evaluation ... \"Time-Optimal Interactive Proofs for Circuit Evaluation.\" arXiv:1304.3812 (2013). https://pith.science/paper/EE7NSFZL\n\nRECORD\n\n# Time-Optimal Interactive Proofs for Circuit Evaluation\n\nPaperPDF from arXiv\n\ndata files\n\nPaper breakdownThe authors' claims, conditions and measurements, as JSON Signed receiptProof this review has not been altered\n\nJustin Thaler\n\nCryptography and Security · Submitted 13 Apr 2013\n\nabstract compiled · 2026-09-23 · no language-model review\n\nThe authors' main claim That is, our prover can evaluate the circuit with a guarantee of correctness, with only a constant-factor blowup in work compared to evaluating the circuit with no guarantee.\n\nhow it is stated · asserted 1.00 read the record →\n\nComment (0) Read on arXiv ↗ Cite ▾\n\narXiv ID1304.3812 (version 4)\n\n[Button: Copy]\n\nPith IDEE7NSFZL, Pith's permanent record of this paper and its review\n\n[Button: Copy]\n\nCitation Justin Thaler. \"Time-Optimal Interactive Proofs for Circuit Evaluation.\" arXiv:1304.3812 (2013). https://pith.science/paper/EE7NSFZL\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmle-pcs/analysis/basefold-analysis.md at main · sec-bit/mle-pcs · GitHub (https://github.com/sec-bit/mle-pcs/blob/main/analysis/basefold-analysis.md)\nciteturn4search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;     sumcheck_sum = UniPolynomial.uni_eval_from_evals(h_evals, alpha, [Fp(0),Fp(1),Fp(2)]) ... where $y_i = h(x_i)$, and $\\omega_i$ are barycentric interpolation weights that can be precomputed:\n    \n    sumcheck_sum = UniPolynomial.uni_eval_from_evals(h_evals, alpha, [Fp(0),Fp(1),Fp(2)])\n    \n    @classmethod\n    def uni_eval_from_evals(cls, evals, z, D):\n        n = len(evals)\n        if n != len(D):\n            raise ValueError(\"Domain size should be equal to the length of evaluations\")\n        if z in D:\n            return evals[D.index(z)]\n        weights = cls.barycentric_weights(D)\n        # print(\"weights={}\".format(weights))\n        e_vec = [weights[i] / (z - D[i]) for i in range(n)]\n        numerator = sum([e_vec[i] * evals[i] for i in range(n)])\n        denominator = sum([e_vec[i] for i in range(n)])\n        return (numerator / denominator)\n\nThis uses barycentric interpolation to compute the value of $h^{(i+1)}(\\alpha_i)$.\n\n$$ h(\\alpha) = \\frac{\\sum \\frac{\\omega_i \\cdot y_i}{\\alpha - x_i}}{\\sum \\frac{\\omega_i}{\\alpha - x_i}} $$\n\nwhere $y_i = h(x_i)$, and $\\omega_i$ are barycentric interpolation weights that can be precomputed:\n\n$$ \\omega_i = \\frac{1}{\\prod_{j=1, j \\neq i}^n (x_i - x_j)} $$\n\nIf $|D| = n$, the computational complexity is:\n\n$$ (2n + 3) ~ \\mathbb{F}_{\\mathsf{mul}} + (n + 2) ~ \\mathbb{F}_{\\mathsf{inv}} $$\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · Plonky3/Plonky3 · GitHub (https://github.com/Plonky3/Plonky3/releases)\nciteturn4search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * perf(multi-stark): precomputed alpha powers, coefficient-wise windows, packed accumulators by @Nashtare in #2014 ...   * perf(sumcheck): bind and measure in one pass by @tcoratger in #2036\n  * fix(ci): retry release-plz on rate limiting by @Nashtare in #2023\n  * perf(fri): closed-form fold_row, in-place fold_matrix, hoisted verifier alpha ladder, fused hiding LDE by @Nashtare in #2015\n  * perf(multi-stark): precomputed alpha powers, coefficient-wise windows, packed accumulators by @Nashtare in #2014\n  * perf(binary-dft): chain butterfly twiddles instead of rebuilding each one by @Nashtare in #2017\n  * test(fri): pin the fused quotient LDE for the bit-reversed DFT backend by @tcoratger in #2025\n  * perf(binary-field): Karatsuba fallback, vector-native GF(2^128) mul, Frobenius conjugation, table squaring by @Nashtare in #2016\n  * refactor(merkle-tree): make the opening shape check self-contained by @tcoratger in #2026\n  * refactor(binary-dft): hoist the twiddle increments out of the stage loop by @tcoratger in #2027\n  * ci: fail on unused dependencies, and drop the ones already there by @tcoratger in #2029\n  * fix(whir): draw independent STIR quer